introduction:
  bigQuestion: "How do simple switches create complex logic?"
  overview: "This unit bridges the gap between raw mathematics and physical hardware. You will learn how computers quantify the world using <strong>Binary</strong>, how simple <strong>Logic Gates</strong> perform decisions, and how the <strong>Operating System</strong> manages these resources to let you run multiple programs at once."
  journey: "You'll start with the basics of data representation (Integers, Hex, ASCII, Images). Then you'll build <strong>Logic Circuits</strong> and simplify them using <strong>Karnaugh Maps</strong>. Finally, you'll explore how the <strong>OS</strong> acts as a manager, scheduling tasks and handling hardware <strong>Interrupts</strong>."

concepts:
  - title: "Data Representation"
    icon: "fa-binary"
    content: |
      <p>Computers only understand 'On' and 'Off'. We use this to represent everything:</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Numbers:</strong> Binary (Base-2) and Hexadecimal (Base-16).</li>
          <li><strong>Text:</strong> ASCII (English only) vs Unicode (Global languages).</li>
          <li><strong>Media:</strong> Bitmaps (Pixels/Color Depth) and Digital Audio (Sampling).</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "Logic Gates & Circuits"
    icon: "fa-microchip"
    content: |
      <p>The building blocks of the CPU. We combine gates to create logic:</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Basic Gates:</strong> AND, OR, NOT.</li>
          <li><strong>Universal Gates:</strong> NAND, NOR (Can build anything).</li>
          <li><strong>Exclusive Gates:</strong> XOR, XNOR.</li>
          <li><strong>Karnaugh Maps:</strong> A graphical way to simplify complex Boolean logic.</li>
      </ul>

  - title: "The Operating System"
    icon: "fa-desktop"
    content: |
      <p>Hardware is useless without software to manage it. The OS handles:</p>
      <ul>
          <li><strong>Process Management:</strong> Scheduling who gets the CPU (Round Robin, FCFS).</li>
          <li><strong>Memory Management:</strong> Using Virtual Memory and Paging.</li>
          <li><strong>Peripheral Management:</strong> Using Drivers and Interrupts.</li>
      </ul>

terminology:
  - term: "Hexadecimal"
    definition: "A base-16 number system (0-9, A-F) used to simplify binary representation for humans."
  - term: "Two's Complement"
    definition: "A method of representing negative integers in binary where the most significant bit is a negative value."
  - term: "Unicode (UTF-8)"
    definition: "A character encoding standard capable of representing all characters in all writing systems of the world."
  - term: "Logic Gate"
    definition: "A physical device implementing a Boolean function (e.g., AND, OR)."
  - term: "Truth Table"
    definition: "A table showing all possible input combinations and their corresponding outputs."
  - term: "Karnaugh Map (K-Map)"
    definition: "A method to simplify boolean algebra expressions using a grid where adjacent cells differ by only one bit."
  - term: "Operating System (OS)"
    definition: "System software that manages computer hardware and software resources and provides common services for programs."
  - term: "Scheduling"
    definition: "The method by which the OS decides which process runs on the CPU and for how long."
  - term: "Interrupt"
    definition: "A signal to the CPU indicating an event that needs immediate attention."
  - term: "Virtual Memory"
    definition: "Using secondary storage (Hard Drive) to act as temporary RAM when main memory is full."

misconceptions:
  - mistake: "Sign & Magnitude vs Two's Complement"
    correction: "Thinking negative binary just adds a '1' at the front. Start from the right, leave the first '1', then flip the rest!"
  - mistake: "Gray Code in K-Maps"
    correction: "Writing headings as 00, 01, 10, 11. WRONG! It must be 00, 01, 11, 10 (Gray Code) or grouping won't work."
  - mistake: "XOR is OR"
    correction: "Thinking XOR means 'one or the other or both'. XOR is strictly 'one or the other, BUT NOT both'."

